A||||10||beer_stud_21||I'm stumped-suggestions please!||||1||
Z||000000||beer_stud_21||05-15-2001||08:48 PM||||Hello all,<P>Well, I've owned my first fiero since november and have yet to drive it or even hear it run!  It's beginning to bum me out.  I've fixed everything that I know to have been broken, but with my limited fiero knolege havn't been able to get the car to run.  I'd like to see if people have suggestions on what I can try next to see if I can't make this thing go.  Here ithe car and what i've done:<P>85GT auto w/70k miles<BR>Hasn't run since middle of 97<BR>Has half a tank of 3 yr old gas tha has been treated with "Seafoam" treatment.<P>I've patched a hole in the fuel line and the engine now gets fuel.  Previous owner put in a brand new coil right before I bought it, I also removed the distributor and replaced the pickup coil.  I've checked for spark and can verify that I do have spark.  (felt it with my own arm.. ;-) ouch.) I can crank the car hard and it will sputter and after a min of this i will smell gas in the exaust but car will not even begin to fire on its own.  I timed it myself by putting #1 cylinder TDC.  Also I have read in other posts that I'm saposed to have A and B terminals connected on the ECM while I do the timing.  Can someone clarify this?  I have 2 fiero manuals but havn't seen anything about this in either of them.  Is this just diaognostic mode, becuase I have had the car in that mode for quite while.  <P>Tell me what you think guys, it's much appreciated!<P>-Marlin||||reg||1
Z||000001||Oreif||05-15-2001||09:00 PM||||You need to put the ECM in "by-pass" mode in order to time the car. (also known as "open loop") This is done by shorting the 2 pins on the ALDL connector located under the cig lighter panel. If it's not in this mode the ECM will try to adjust the timing and you'll never get it set correctly.<BR>As for the running problem, you could have clogged injectors. If the injectors are clogged/dirty/gummed up, they will "spit" the gas out instead of spraying. This will flood the car quickly. The Fiero's use a needle pin for the injectors which can gum up/clog easily. The 3.1L engines use a disk type of injector head and don't get clogged as easily. A clogged cat, bad fuel regulator, or a clogged IAC valve will also cause a high rich/no start condition also.<P>------------------<BR>     <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/LOGO3.jpg">   <BR>Got Emblems?||||reg||1
Z||000002||Jason Kamrud||05-15-2001||09:13 PM||||Have you tried squirting gas into the intake? If it sputters more than without squirting gas, you've got some fuel delivery trouble-clogged line/filter, injectors, or not pumping.  I believe there are two fuel pumps(one supply pump & one high-pressure), and they both have to operate at the same time for enough pressure to shoot through the injectors. The relays for these pumps are inside the engine compartment on the firewall I believe, and the pumps are inside the gas tank.<P>One way to test fuel pressure is to turn on the key so the pump runs a few seconds, and on the fuel rail near the steel fuel lines is a little valve stem-lookin bleeder thing. Thread the cap off & push the pin in with a screwdriver, and if there is good fuel pressure the gas will squirt directly into your eyes, so watch out.<P>Give it a try & see what happens.<P>------------------<BR>Jason Kamrud||||reg||1
Z||000003||87GTZ34||05-15-2001||09:18 PM||||Oreif is correct.  Diagnostic mode will take the variables out of the computer (to modify timing) and allow you to 'see' base timing on your timing light.  You should set the timing to 10 degrees b4 TDC and not TDC.  Also I would recommend that you siphon out the old gas, put in 5 gallons of fresh gas along with the proper amount of high strength injector cleaner/lube.  You may also want to change the fuel filter first to make sure you are getting all the pressure & flow possible from your system.<P>GL||||reg||1
Z||000004||Raydar||05-15-2001||09:32 PM||||Please forgive me if you have already checked this...<BR>Are you sure that #1 is at TDC on the <I>compression</I> stroke, instead of exhaust/intake? <BR>It's an easy mistake to make.<BR>You might try turning the engine one revolution, and set the distributor, again. <P>If you smell gas, it usually means that it's flooded. <BR>Try holding the gas all the way to the floor while you crank it. If everything is set correctly, but it's flooded, it should eventually chug to life. As the engine "clears its throat" it should speed up and run.<P>------------------<BR>Raydar - aka Steve<P>88 black Formula<BR>88 Duke coupe <BR>88 "wadded up" Formula (parts car)<p>[This message has been edited by Raydar (edited 05-15-2001).]||||reg||5
Z||000005||beer_stud_21||05-15-2001||10:26 PM||||Thanks for your replys guys.  I have replaced the fuel filter already.  The seafoam is saposed to clean the injectors, but I dunno how well it has worked.  Also I did not/have not timed the car with a timing light.  Only done it by hand and have done it twice to make sure it was on compression stroke.  I also disconnected the fuel line near the battery after I had treated the gas in the tank with seafoam so that hopfully the treated gas would get to the tank and clean out the injectors etc...<P>thanks for your help guys, keep em coming.  I might get to hear this thing run afterall...<P>-Marlin||||reg||1
Z||000006||PBJ||05-15-2001||10:36 PM||||extended cranking and flooding can "wash" the oil off the cylender walls-leading to little, to no compression. Remove the spark plugs and squirt a bit of oil in each cylender-clean and dry spark plugs before putting them back in.||||reg||1
Z||000007||Vader||05-15-2001||10:45 PM||||Seafoam is supposed to clean the injectors?  And mystics are supposed to see the future, but that doesn't work either.<P>The only way to properly clean and flow test an injector (or set of injectors) is to ultrasonically clean them in a bath of butyl cellusolve or hexane.  This will remove all traces of varnish and fuel additive deposits and allow full flow and adequate pintle sealing.  Fuel additives are maybe good as a preventive measure, but once an injector is clogging, it's usually too late for any "miracle in a can" to do much good.<P>You should probably install clean, dry spark plugs to help get you started, unplug the injector electrical connector, and hand prime the TB with a small pump bottle of fuel.  A Plews-type oiler works well for this.  <P>The three-year old fuel has probably lost almost all volatility as well, and should be freshened with a lot of alcohol or drained and replaced.<P>Good luck.<P>------------------<BR>"<I>Make Me Bad</I>"||||reg||1
Z||000008||Ken Wittlief||05-16-2001||12:18 AM||||I dont understand what you mean by "i disconnected the fuel line by the battery" ?!?<P> the fuel runs from the tank through the pump, through the fuel rails, through the pressure regulator, and back to the tank. If you have the return line disconnected and blocked off, then your fuel pressure regulator cant work, and your fuel pressure will be way too high, and will flood the engine.<P>  also, just because you got a jolt from the spark plug wire doesnt mean you have good spark. Pull the plugs and clean them with throttle body cleaner (takes carbon right off) or replace them, and hold the spark plug wire with a rag while someone cranks the engine and make sure you get a nice blue spark.<P>  also check and make sure the plug wires are on in the correct firing order. When I put my new plug wires on, the diagram in the book was such that I could have taken either of two leads on the cap to be plug one, the cap was several degrees off from how the picture looks.<P>  If you have compression and spark, and you smell gas in the exhaust, then it ought to at least fire up, it may run like crap, but it should at least putter or something.||||reg||1
Z||000009||Indiana_resto_guy||05-16-2001||01:08 AM||||Just a few points to consider doing.<P>1. Check fuses for injectors in fuse box.<BR>2. Do a fuel pressure check.<BR>3. Check all spark plugs for proper gap and general condition, if worn out or discolored from 'normal', replace them.<BR>4. Inspect plug wires for cracks, worn boots (both ends) and corrosion inside at contacts.<BR>If found bad or questionable condition, replace.<BR>5. Inspect closely rotor and cap condition and excessive build up of oxidized material, cracks and discolored appearance. Replace as necessary.<P>Also the correct mark on the crankshaft pulley is the WIDEST of the 3. I suggest to locate it and mark it for high visibility.<P><BR>A good way to set timing static is to turn crank to compression stroke for #1 cylinder. Stop at 10 degrees before TDC and set the distributors rotor to #1 spark plug wire on the cap with the flat side of the distributor (where the pick-up module's plugs are) parallel with the mating surfaces of engine in relationship to transmission.<BR>Depending on which book your using, Haynes chapter 1, section 38 (page 53) explains using the diagnostic terminals A and B. (diagnostic mode)<P>Using Chiltons chapter 2 (page 46) IGNITION TIMING, explains how to time engine by disconnecting the 4 terminal connector at the distributor.<P>If the problem persists after these tests check ALL (including cold start)injectors for proper operation. Make sure that when #1 is up on compression stroke that the valves are closed. This can be done with a compression gauge. If it builds the valves are closed, if not the timing chain may be bad. The only reason that I say this is because I don't know the history of the car.<P>Change gas and treat as suggested by 87GTZ34||||reg||1
Z||000010||Ken Wittlief||05-16-2001||10:02 AM||||Thats a good point - look under the oil fill cap while someone cranks the engine - make sure the cam is spinnig.<P> a little encouragement for you: when I went down (70 miles away) to look at the car I have now, it wasnt running. The owner said it had started up two weeks before, but now it would only fire once or twice and then nothing. <P>  We replaced the cap and rotor, it looked original (107k on it). Still nothing. I had to head back home, so the owner replace the plug wires on the back three cylinders. Still nothing. finally he pulled the plugs out and replaced them. It started up immediately and ran on 3 cylinders.<P>   He called me and said, "Ken, what a couple of dopes we are - we skipped the most obvious thing - fouled up plugs"<P>  I drove back down and put the other 3 plugs and wires in, and the rest is history.<BR> <BR>  dont get discouraged. Usually things that completely stop an engine from running are simple things.||||reg||1
